# Model card for reginute1 (medium)

* Language: lt_LT (Lithuanian, Lithuania)
* Speakers: 1
* Quality: medium
* Samplerate: 22,050Hz

## Dataset

* URL: https://huggingface.co/datasets/meldynamics/liepa-tts
* License: CC-BY-4.0

LIEPA corpus, Vilnius University. One speaker — a professional actress, not
named here on purpose (the licence credits the publishers, not the person) —
studio recordings, 5 121 utterances, ~3 hours. Stress and pitch
accents in the training transcriptions come from the corpus' own annotation,
sentence by sentence.

## Training

Fine-tuned from the Piper catalogue checkpoint `ru_RU-irina-medium`
(`rhasspy/piper-checkpoints`), which is itself fine-tuned from
`en_US-lessac-medium`. Training data licence is CC-BY-4.0; the lineage above is
stated so that the base-model question (see piper-voices discussion #94) is
visible rather than hidden.

Released checkpoint: **epoch 9193, val_mel 0.3603** (training stopped 2026-09-06,
the last ~900 epochs moved val_mel by 0.0015 and the ear no longer heard a
difference). The training checkpoint is shipped alongside the model
(`lt_LT-reginute1-medium-e9193.ckpt`, 269 MB,
optimizer state stripped) so that another Lithuanian voice can start from this
one rather than from a Russian checkpoint. Both the generator and the
discriminator are included, since a GAN needs both to continue training.

## Phonemization — please read

This voice uses `phoneme_type: text` with a custom `phoneme_id_map`
(IPA + three Lithuanian accent marks `ˈ ˌ ˋ`). It does **not** use espeak-ng at
synthesis time, because espeak-ng misplaces Lithuanian stress in roughly half
of the words against the corpus' gold annotation.

Text must therefore be phonemized by `phonemize_lithuanian.py` (shipped with
the voice; submitted as a `PhonemeType.LITHUANIAN` contribution to piper1-gpl)
together with the stress dictionary `lt_kirciai.tsv`. Plain Piper without the
phonemizer feeds the model raw letters and will not produce Lithuanian.

Two configs exist for the same model, on purpose:

* **piper-voices catalogue:** `"phoneme_type": "lithuanian"` — Piper runs the
  phonemizer itself (needs a Piper that includes the piper1-gpl contribution;
  an older Piper refuses to load the voice with a clear error rather than
  producing noise). Put `lt_kirciai.tsv` from this repo next to the model or
  pass `--data-dir`; the catalogue folder holds only the model and config,
  like every other voice.
* **this repo / older Piper:** `"phoneme_type": "text"` — you phonemize with
  the module and pass IPA to Piper (`synth_reginute.py` does exactly that).

This is the same arrangement as the Hebrew (piper1-gpl #244), Chinese pinyin
(#271) and Japanese (#274) voices: the phoneme type names a phonemizer inside
Piper, and the catalogue folder holds only the model and the config. See the
voice repo README, section "For catalogue maintainers", for the merge order
and the two precedents for shipping the dictionary.

The module also normalizes text first (`skaiciu_pletiklis.py`, picked up
automatically when present, `expand_text=None` to disable): Lithuanian number
case endings, clock times, units and letter-by-letter abbreviations. Without
it espeak-ng reads `5000 eurų` as "penki tūkstantčei" and `15:00` as
"tūkstantis penkišimtai:".

Recommended: `normalize_audio=False` (the default `True` clips this voice).

## Known limitations

Listed because you will meet them anyway, and finding them yourself after
being told everything is fine is worse than reading them here.

* **Single short words come out hurried and thin.** The corpus is made of
  sentences: only 93 of its 5 121 utterances are short standalone ones, and
  the word "du" (two) appears alone exactly once — as a question. Numbers
  read one at a time are the worst case; inside a sentence the same words are
  fine. A short phrase ending in a full stop is a pattern the model has
  barely seen.
* **The stress dictionary has individual errors.** It is derived, not
  hand-checked word by word. Two confirmed: `procentas` and
  `kilovatvalandės` carry the stress on the wrong syllable, and in both
  cases the word family disagrees with itself (`procentas` vs
  `procentinis`). Expect more of the same kind, mostly in loanwords and
  compounds.
* **Homographs are not solved and cannot be, by a dictionary.** Lithuanian
  `nãmo` ("of the house") and `namõ` ("homewards") are spelled identically
  and differ only in accent. A word-level lookup has no way to choose; that
  needs sentence context.
* **The voice needs the phonemizer.** Plain `piper -m lt_LT-reginute1-medium.onnx`
  will not speak Lithuanian — see the phonemization section above. This is
  the one limitation that is architectural rather than a rough edge.
* **`length_scale` is a judgement, not a measurement.** The default 1.30 was
  chosen by ear against recordings of the original speaker. Other listeners
  may prefer faster; override it freely. (For what it is worth, the same
  listener later compared the two side by side against the original speaker
  reading the same text: this voice came out 1.2 % slower than her, so the
  setting is at least not far off.)

None of these block ordinary use — the voice reads news, answers a smart
speaker and speaks long articles without a break. They are listed so that
you know what you are getting.

